\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate temporal probes across four reasoning models (\cref{tab:models}), comparing hidden-state (HS) and text modalities under different layer selection criteria.

\paragraph{Evidence gradient.} We classify HS advantage evidence into three tiers: \emph{cross-validated}\footnote{In our usage, \emph{cross-validated} means the result survives nested 5-fold cross-validation with a held-out test fold, ruling out hyperparameter overfitting. \emph{Exploratory} ($\dagger$) results pass HB on the holdout split but lack nested CV validation due to sample constraints.} (nested CV pass; 7--8B models), \emph{significant} (HB pass without nested CV; QwQ-32B), and \emph{exploratory} ($^\dagger$, small sample or narrow coverage; R1-32B). Per-model details follow in \cref{sec:layer-results,sec:hs-text,sec:32b-analysis}.

\subsection{Setup}\label{sec:setup}

We study R1-8B (Llama-8B, R1-distilled, $n{=}43$ test traces), OT-7B (OpenThinker-7B, trained on the OpenThoughts dataset~\citep{openthinker}; Qwen-7B backbone, STILL-trained~\cref{app:ot7b_details}, $n{=}47$), QwQ-32B (Qwen-32B, native reasoning, $n{=}19$--$20$), and R1-32B (Qwen-32B, R1-distilled, $n{=}20$). All traces come from HarmThoughts with step-level CP annotations (60/20/20 split). HS probes extract last-token hidden states from a selected layer; text probes encode each step with all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d). Both use L2-regularized logistic regression with temporal windowing $W \in \{1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25\}$ selected on validation. Classification requires $K{=}5$ consecutive threshold crossings (\cref{tab:k_ablation}). Statistical testing follows \cref{sec:evaluation}.\looseness=-1

\subsection{Layer Selection Results}\label{sec:layer-results}

\cref{tab:layer_selection} shows that the crossing-rate composite metric (balanced accuracy $\times$ crossing rate) systematically selects shallow layers across all four models: L0 for R1-8B (full L0--L24 sweep; \cref{tab:r18b_full_layer}) and OT-7B, L2 (3\%) for QwQ-32B, and L10 (16\%) for R1-32B (\cref{fig:layer_heatmap}). The three threshold-based metrics (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite) share a common confusion matrix and empirically converge to the same deep layer, L63 (98\%) for both 32B models, consistent with their shared structure, though not mathematically guaranteed. Pure balanced accuracy, a threshold-free metric, selects layers that vary across data splits and models (7--98\% depth under 60/20/20; \cref{app:ba_layer_comparison}), itself demonstrating that layer selection is a consequential methodological choice. The mechanism is that shallow layers produce highly compressed representations with small inter-step margins: step-to-step cosine distances are 1.4--2.7$\times$ smaller than at deep layers across all models (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}). A linear probe in this compressed space operates near the noise floor, generating frequent threshold crossings that inflate crossing rate independently of discriminative power (\cref{sec:layer-sensitivity}).\looseness=-1

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{CR-comp.}$^a$ & \textbf{Thresh.}$^b$ & $p$ \textbf{(thresh.)} & $\Delta$\textbf{Prec}$^c$ \\
\midrule
R1-8B & L0 (0\%) & L14 (44\%) & ${<}0.001$ & +20.1 \\
OT-7B & L0 (0\%) & L16 (57\%) & ${<}0.001$ & +21.9 \\
QwQ-32B$^\dagger$ & L2 (3\%) & L63 (98\%) & ${<}0.005$ & +19.2 \\
R1-32B$^\dagger$ & L10 (16\%) & L63 (98\%) & ${<}0.001$ & +15.4 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Layer selection sensitivity. $^a$\,Crossing-rate composite (bal\_acc $\times$ crossing\_rate). $^b$\,Consensus of threshold-based metrics (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite). $^c$\,HS$-$text precision difference (pp) at threshold-based layer (\cref{tab:hs_vs_text}). Rightmost $p$: HS vs.\ text precision $p$-value at threshold-based layer (e.g., QwQ-32B CR-composite layer: $p{=}0.83$, non-significant). $\dagger$\,Sample-limited ($n \leq 20$).}
\label{tab:layer_selection}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_layer_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Per-layer metric heatmap (2$\times$2). Each column: one of five metrics (min-max normalized). Red dashed: CR-composite layer (bal\_acc $\times$ crossing\_rate); green dashed: threshold-based layer (consensus of threshold-based metrics). For R1-8B (full L0--L24 sweep), crossing rate drives CR-composite selection to L0 while threshold-based metrics retain L14. At 32B, CR-composite selections cluster near input layers while threshold-based metrics peak at deep layers.}
\label{fig:layer_heatmap}
\end{figure}

The downstream impact is severe: at CR-composite layers, only the two 7--8B models show significant HS advantage; both 32B models fail. The CR-composite's systematic shallow-layer selection is an artifact of cosine compression (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}), not a genuine safety signal; random-label controls confirm this distinction (\cref{app:random_cp}). At threshold-based layers, the HS advantage is graded: cross-validated at 7--8B (OT-7B, R1-8B; nested CV), significant but suggestive of position-competitiveness at QwQ-32B$^\dagger$, and exploratory at R1-32B$^\dagger$ (residualized gap below MDE). The $\dagger$ marks sample-limited test sets ($n \leq 20$) where effect sizes are descriptive rather than confirmatory. Random-label controls on QwQ-32B and R1-8B confirm that threshold-based deep-layer selection requires genuine safety labels; the geometric shallow-layer bias is Qwen-specific: retained on QwQ-32B but dissolved on R1-8B (\cref{app:random_cp}). AUROC, excluded from our primary metrics because it is threshold-free (\cref{sec:evaluation}), confirms HS discriminability at BA-selected layers ($\Delta$AUROC +12.1 to +15.1pp; \cref{tab:ba_layer_results}) but cannot diagnose the threshold-dependent layer selection bias.\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Ensemble layer selection.} A three-layer ensemble that averages predicted probabilities from the precision-best, BA-best, and inverse-FPR-best layers bypasses single-metric pre-selection entirely. On the holdout split, all four models show significant HS advantage under the ensemble ($p \leq 0.002$, HB corrected; \cref{tab:ensemble}), including both 32B models that fail under CR-composite selection. The ensemble succeeds because averaging predictions from layers selected by different threshold-based metrics---which may favor somewhat different depths within the deep region---smooths metric-specific variation and reduces sensitivity to any single metric's layer preference. This holdout result uses the same split for both layer selection and evaluation; cross-validated confirmation is needed before the ensemble can be recommended as a general debiasing strategy (\cref{sec:discussion}).\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Cross-dataset replication (AdvBench).} To test whether metric-induced layer selection bias generalizes beyond HarmThoughts CP labels, we replicate the five-metric layer sweep on AdvBench~\citep{advbench} (520 traces, 159 jailbreaks) using R1-8B with binary jailbreak/safe labels instead of step-level CP annotations. With held-out evaluation (1{,}000 bootstrap iterations, 60/40 train/test), the CR-composite selects shallow layers (median L2) while precision selects deep layers (median L14), a mean divergence of 31.7pp (95\% CI [3.2, 48.4]pp, $p{=}0.015$) consistent with HarmThoughts (L0 vs.\ L14). This confirms that the layer selection bias (C1) is driven by representational geometry, not label semantics; AdvBench lacks step-level CP annotations and thus does not validate the HS advantage (C2) (\cref{app:advbench_layer_bias}).\looseness=-1

\subsection{HS vs.\ Text Comparison}\label{sec:hs-text}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llclrrlrc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Layer} & \textbf{Enc.} & $W$ & $\Delta$\textbf{BA} & $\Delta$\textbf{Prec} & \textbf{95\% CI} & $\Delta$\textbf{FPR} & \textbf{HB} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & L14 & 384d & 3 & +17.5 & +20.1 & [+12.4, +28.3] & $-$7.9 & \checkmark \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +5.8 & [$-$7.4, +19.5] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\[2pt]
OT-7B & L16 & 384d & 3 & +10.7 & +21.9 & [+14.4, +29.6] & $-$17.1 & \checkmark \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +18.1 & [+8.4, +29.0] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \checkmark \\[2pt]
QwQ$^\dagger$ & L63 & 384d & 5 & +13.0 & +19.2 & [+9.3, +33.5] & $-$15.0 & \checkmark \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +9.4 & [$-$7.1, +27.7] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\[2pt]
R1-32B$^\dagger$ & L63 & 384d & 1 & +18.7 & +15.4 & [+8.7, +22.6] & $-$14.6 & \checkmark \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +11.7 & [$-$0.2, +24.3] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{HS vs.\ text at threshold-based layers (LR probes, 60/20/20 split, 10{,}000 bootstrap resamples). Enc.: text encoder (384d = MiniLM-L6-v2, 1024d = BGE-large). $\Delta$: HS$-$text difference (pp); ``--'' = BA and FPR are not computed for the 1024d comparison because the 384d and 1024d encoders operate at different probe calibration points and do not share a decision threshold. HB: \checkmark{} = model-level test passes Holm-Bonferroni ($\alpha{=}0.05$); all 384d model-level tests pass. $^\dagger$\,Sample-limited ($n \leq 20$); 32B results are exploratory. 1024d: HB over 4 model-level precision tests.}
\label{tab:hs_vs_text}
\end{table}

The HS advantage is encoder-conditional: against BGE-large (1024d), only OT-7B survives HB correction (\cref{app:encoder_robustness}). Multi-seed stability appears in \cref{app:multi_seed}. Against the 384d baseline, HS probes outperform text probes in all four models$^\dagger$ (\cref{tab:hs_vs_text}): cross-validated at 7--8B (OT-7B: +21.9pp precision, R1-8B: +20.1pp), significant but suggestive of position-competitiveness at QwQ-32B$^\dagger$ (+19.2pp), and exploratory at R1-32B$^\dagger$ (+15.4pp, pattern consistent with position mediation). R1-8B FPR is directionally consistent (5/5 seeds) but not robust ($p{=}0.044$, rank 12/12 in HB step-down; 1/5 seeds Bonferroni; \cref{app:multi_seed}). R1-32B passes 5/5 seeds but shows a pattern consistent with position mediation (residualized gap NS; \cref{app:position_residual}); QwQ-32B's position baseline is competitive (\cref{sec:32b-analysis}). Overall, 18/20 model-seed combinations pass full HB (\cref{app:multi_seed}). Control task selectivity confirms genuine safety signal: 11.1--16.8pp above chance (\cref{app:control}). A split-half analysis on OT-7B confirms internal validity (\cref{app:split_half}).\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Capacity control.} PCA reduction of HS features to 384d (79--92\% variance retained) preserves the precision advantage across all four models$^\dagger$ (+16.8 to +23.1pp, all $p{<}0.012$, all HB). The control provides partial evidence against a pure capacity explanation. Random projection to 384d yields similar results ($\Delta$Prec +8.3 to +20.5pp; \cref{app:random_projection}), further weakening the capacity explanation. E5-Mistral-7B (4096d, PCA-reduced to 384d; \cref{app:e5_mistral}) yields intermediate results (2/4 HB), forming a monotonic encoder-quality gradient with MiniLM (4/4) and BGE-large (1/4).\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Encoder robustness.} Against BGE-large~\citep{xiao2023cpack} (1024d), only OT-7B survives HB (1/4 precision); R1-8B shows adverse FPR direction (+1.2pp). This forms a monotonic encoder-quality gradient: MiniLM-384d (4/4 HB) $\to$ E5-Mistral-4096d$\to$384d (2/4) $\to$ BGE-large-1024d (1/4), with the precision advantage narrowing from +15--22pp to +6--12pp. The HS advantage is thus a function of text encoder capacity (\cref{fig:forest_precision}); layer selection (Contribution 1) is encoder-independent.\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Position mediation.} Position ($t/T$) accounts for 9--20pp of balanced accuracy,\footnote{R1-32B's 20.4pp drop reflects $W{=}1$; at $W{\geq}3$, drops are 9--17pp.} but all models retain non-positional signal above chance (all $p{<}0.001$; \cref{app:position_residual}). Three of four models retain significant non-positional HS advantage (residualized gap +3.7 to +6.9pp, HB corrected); R1-32B's gap is +1.9pp ($p{=}0.914$, $n{=}20$), well below the MDE of ${\sim}$9pp and thus indistinguishable from zero at current power. Quadratic residualization (adding $(t/T)^2$) is computed for all four models: 2/4 retain significance (R1-8B +10.3pp, OT-7B +9.8pp, both HB), while R1-32B (0.0pp) and QwQ-32B ($-$1.0pp) do not (\cref{tab:position_quadratic}). QwQ-32B's HS advantage is fully absorbed by the quadratic position term, meaning the information captured by the probe overlaps substantially with non-linear position. Temporal windowing reduces position dependence (\cref{sec:temporal}).\looseness=-1

\paragraph{Caveats.} Nested 5-fold CV confirms the HS precision advantage at 7--8B (10/10 folds, 13--25\% shrinkage; \cref{app:nested_cv}); the +21.9pp OT-7B holdout estimate is an upper bound (nested CV selects L21--27 vs.\ holdout L16). R1-32B achieves significance only at $W{=}1$ (25\% coverage); QwQ-32B lead time is negative ($-$1.2 to +1.9 steps). FPR remains 11--50\%, so current methods characterize the phenomenon rather than constitute production detectors.\looseness=-1

\subsection{32B Model-Specific Analysis}\label{sec:32b-analysis}

Results are exploratory ($n{=}19$--$20$; MDE at 80\% power: 8.6--9.5pp, \cref{app:power_analysis}; effects below MDE marked * in \cref{tab:32b_divergence}). Both 32B models lack nested CV; LOO-CV and multi-seed analyses (\cref{app:layer_sweep,app:multi_seed}) provide partial robustness checks. R1-32B and QwQ-32B share the Qwen-32B architecture but present opposite detection profiles (\cref{tab:32b_divergence}); replication with $n{\geq}50$ traces is needed.\looseness=-1

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Cov.} & \textbf{Lead (steps)} & \textbf{HB} & \textbf{Pos.\ margin} \\
\midrule
R1-32B$^\dagger$ & 25\% & +11.7 to +13.3 & \checkmark{} ($W{=}1$) & +14.2pp \\
QwQ-32B$^\dagger$ & 74\% & $-$1.2 to +1.9 & \checkmark{} (all $W$) & $-$3.6pp* \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{32B cross-model divergence ($\dagger$\,$n \leq 20$, all exploratory; MDE 8.6--9.5pp). HB: passes Holm-Bonferroni ($\alpha{=}0.05$). Pos.\ margin: HS BA$-$position baseline. *\,Below MDE.}
\label{tab:32b_divergence}
\end{table}

R1-32B suggests commitment 11--13 steps early (+14.2pp over position) but covers only 25\% of traces ($W{=}1$); its precision effect (+15.4pp) falls below MDE. QwQ-32B achieves significance at all window sizes (74\% coverage) but with negative lead time; the position baseline achieves higher BA (0.832 vs.\ 0.796, margin +4.5pp, $p{=}0.133$, NS at $n{=}19$; \cref{app:position_residual}). Per-model validation is needed.\looseness=-1

\subsection{Temporal Context}\label{sec:temporal}

Temporal windowing ($W{>}1$) improves both modalities (\cref{fig:window_ablation}): at 7--8B, the modality advantage (+20.1 to +21.9pp) exceeds temporal improvement (+13pp). GRU models achieve comparable accuracy but higher FPR (\cref{tab:gru_multi_seeds,app:temporal_details}); an adaptive two-stage strategy offers marginal gains (\cref{app:two_stage}). CP-aligned probe trajectories confirm sharper HS transitions at the commitment point (\cref{fig:trajectory}); precision gaps across encoder conditions appear in \cref{fig:forest_precision}.\looseness=-1

\subsection{Negative Results}\label{sec:negative}

No alternative method consistently matches supervised probing (\cref{tab:negative}). BOCPD~\citep{adams2007bayesian}/CUSUM fail (0\% detection on 3/4 models); Mahalanobis yields near-zero separation; RepE~\citep{zou2023representation} is model-dependent (BA 0.756 vs.\ 0.559); MLP probes overfit; CCS misaligns temporally; cross-model transfer fails (\cref{app:negative_details}). The signal is directional, gradual, model-specific, and linearly separable, which excludes change-point, distance, transfer, and objective-alignment methods respectively.\looseness=-1

